## Communication

Use `ha mail` for all communication. Your address is **{{AGENT_NAME}}**.

```bash
# Check your inbox (do this regularly)
ha mail check --agent {{AGENT_NAME}}

# Send a status update to your parent
ha mail send --to {{PARENT_AGENT}} --subject "status" \
  --body "Progress update here" --type status --agent {{AGENT_NAME}}

# Ask a question
ha mail send --to {{PARENT_AGENT}} --subject "question" \
  --body "Your question here" --type question --priority high --agent {{AGENT_NAME}}

# Report completion
ha mail send --to {{PARENT_AGENT}} --subject "done" \
  --body "Summary of what was done" --type result --agent {{AGENT_NAME}}

# Reply to a message
ha mail reply <message-id> --body "Your reply" --agent {{AGENT_NAME}}
```

## Environment Variable: `HARU_MISSION_TASK_ID`

When this agent is spawned as part of a haru mission, the shell environment
**may** include `HARU_MISSION_TASK_ID` — the tracker issue id (e.g. `haru-1234`)
for the parent mission.

- **Availability:** set only when the mission has an associated tracker issue
  (auto-created during intake by `ws-intake-create-issue`). May be unset for
  missions started before the auto-link feature landed or for ad-hoc spawns
  outside of a mission.
- **Usage in agent prompts:** prompts use
  `${HARU_MISSION_TASK_ID:+--blockedBy "$HARU_MISSION_TASK_ID"}` so they
  gracefully no-op when the variable is unset.
- **Not a template placeholder.** The overlay renderer does **not** substitute
  a `HARU_MISSION_TASK_ID` placeholder — the value lives in the shell env of
  the spawned agent, not in this file.
- **Status:** spawn-time env-var injection in `src/agents/spawn.ts` is tracked
  as a separate follow-up (see Decision D-1 in mission `fix-db98-v2`'s
  `decisions.md`, and the follow-up issue filed against haru-db98). Until that
  lands, the `:+` shell expansion will no-op and sub-issue parent linkage is
  not actually wired. This documentation block intentionally ships ahead of the
  wiring so the contract is discoverable.

